// Book Four, Chapter 1 (session 40). The seventh waking, in November 1859.
// Reads the chained wake report (place, steward, outcome, orders) and plays a waking for each. Woken early gets its own opening and a short trouble scene.
// Order: waking, hunger, the steward's report (with the Emperor beat), then one night phase of three actions out of four, then dawn and the road to the sea.
// He wakes in Paris (the haven under the shop, or the tomb) or on the hill in Tuscany, so the chapter ends with rest() and => next into chapter 2 (the crossing and New Orleans).
// The coven fronts the waking: business_lead() (the member best at business) holds the lantern, gives the report and lays out the desk (b4.lead_desk, b4.lead_pick). Without a coven the old stewards speak as before.
// Reads wake(), b3.keeper, b3.crane_offer and the circle. Sets b4.lead_desk, b4.lead_pick, b4.village (the hill's letter), b4.trouble, b4.first_feed, b4.emperor, b4.books, b4.estate, b4.street, b4.circle, b4.crane_reply and b4.passage.
@chapter b4c01
@title A Letter from the River
@book house-divided
@version 3

# start
## The seventh waking
$ b4.trouble = "none"
$ b4.lead_desk = "approved"
$ b4.lead_pick = "none"
$ b4.village = "waited"
$ b4.first_feed = "animal"
$ b4.emperor = "bluffed"
$ b4.books = "trusted"
$ b4.estate = "unseen"
$ b4.street = "watched"
$ b4.circle = "stay"
$ b4.crane_reply = "burned"
$ b4.passage = "cabin"
$ _hill = 0
$ _reader = "Anselme's grandson"
$ _lead = ""
?if wake("place") == "collesanto"
  $ _hill = 1
?end
?if wake("steward") == "keepers"
  $ _reader = "Filippo"
?elif wake("steward") == "adelaide" or wake("steward") == "baptiste"
  $ _reader = "Honoré"
?elif wake("steward") == "prisca"
  $ _reader = "Prisca"
?end
?if coven_size() > 0 and wake("by") != "steward"
  $ _lead = business_lead()
?end
?if _lead != ""
  $ _reader = name(_lead)
  $ do meet(_lead)
?end
?if _reader == "Honoré"
  $ do meet("honore")
?elif _reader == "Filippo"
  $ do meet("filippo")
?end
~> waking
?if wake("outcome") == "woken_early"
  ~> trouble
?end
?if must_feed()
  ~> hunger
?end
---
~> report
---
?if _lead != ""
  ~> desk
  $ do appoint_lead()
  ---
?end
$ do phase("night", 3)
It is a night in the first week of November, in the year eighteen hundred and fifty nine, and there are, by your own reckoning, four things in it that want doing and time for three. {cap(voice("hunger"))}.
-> night

# waking
?if wake("outcome") == "woken_early"
  ?if wake("by") == "hunters"
    You do not wake so much as get interrupted. There is a scrape of iron on stone, and a gout of lantern light, and the loud, cheerful, careful voices of several men who have bought a map in a café and mean to be worth the price of it. They are not hunters of your kind. They are hunters of a kind that the age has produced in great numbers, a sort of gentleman who goes down into the earth with a hamper to look at bones and old gold, and they have found a door with no latch, behind a wall that was supposed to be solid.
    You are a great deal hungrier than you have ever been, and every one of them is warm.
  ?elif wake("by") == "government"
    You wake to a cough, a polite one, and the smell of lamp oil and wet plaster. There is a gentleman at the foot of the bed in a good gray overcoat, and a clerk behind him with a measuring chain, and two laborers who are looking at the ceiling with the particular blankness of men who have found something and do not want to be asked. The gentleman is a commissioner of works. He has a plan, in a tube, and a list, and the room that you are lying in is on the list, in red.
    "Forgive me," he says. "I am obliged to ask, in the name of the Prefect, who is the owner of this cellar."
  ?else
    Somebody is shaking you. It is a thing that nobody has done in a very long time, and it is being done by {_reader}, in a traveling coat, with the face of someone who has come a long way to do something that he has been telling himself for a week is the right thing. "Forgive me, Monsieur," you hear. "I was to wake you if the house was threatened. I think that it is threatened. I think that I may be the threat."
    A gentleman came to the counting house in the spring, it seems. He gave no name, and could not afterward be described. He asked for a small favor, a letter carried to a certain address with a key folded in it, and it was done, and then there was a long, cold, sleepless week.
  ?end
?else
  ?if wake("place") == "collesanto"
    You wake in the cellar on the hill, and the first thing that you know is that the air is warm and smells of woodsmoke and wet slate. The bar on the inside of the door has been oiled. There is a lamp, a good one, of a pattern that you do not recognize, and a chair, and a loaf under a cloth, and through the stone above you, very faint, a harmonium and a great many people kneeling at once. You have been put to bed in the middle of a saint's day. You gather, from the volume, that the saint is you.
  ?elif wake("place") == "haven"
    You wake in the cellar under the shop, and the first thing that you know is the smell: paper, lamp oil, damp, and under it a sweetness that you take a moment to name, which is coal gas. The floor above you is shaking, gently and without pause, like a ship's. It is not the presses. The presses have a rhythm. This is a great deal larger and has none, a dull, continual, far-off thudding, as if somebody were taking down a city with a spoon.
  ?else
    You wake in the tomb, and the stone is moved. Cold, clean, stale air comes in, with a slice of lantern light in it and a smell of lime. It is a chamber cut in the rock under the faubourg, with no door but the one, and somebody has taken a great deal of care to open it from the right side, and a good deal less ceremony than you would have liked.
  ?end
  ?if _lead != ""
    ~> lead_waking
  ?elif wake("steward") == "prisca"
    The person holding the lantern is small and exact, in a traveling cloak of excellent cloth, with a steel pen behind one ear and an abacus in her sleeve. She does not breathe. She has not, for some time, bothered to pretend to. "Sixty five years and four months," says Prisca. "It is a very awkward number. I should like it understood that I have been keeping the books for the whole of it and that I have enjoyed perhaps one afternoon."
  ?elif wake("steward") == "keepers"
    The man holding the lantern is lean and brown, in a black coat, with the habit of looking at the floor first. He has Aelia's chin. You have seen that chin on a hundred faces in a thousand years, and every time it goes through you like a nail. "Filippo Valerio," he says, in the Tuscan of a gentleman farmer, with a small stiff bow. "Of the hill. Forgive me, Signore. I was taught what to say, and my father taught me, and his mother taught him, and I have been saying it to the wall for a month in case I forgot."
  ?elif wake("steward") == "adelaide" or wake("steward") == "baptiste"
    The man holding the lantern is fifty, stout, with ink in the creases of both thumbs and a pair of spectacles pushed up into hair the color of a pewter pot. He has Adélaïde's eyes, or Baptiste's chin, depending on the day. "Honoré Valère," he says, "printer, of the rue Saint-Jacques, and the great-grandson of somebody whose name you will know. Forgive me, Monsieur. The house has kept to its instructions for three generations, and I may be the first that has had to carry them out, and I am afraid that I have rather lost the paper they were on."
  ?else
    The young man holding the lantern is plump and pink, in a good brown coat, with a small iron bound purse in the blue of the Merchant house on his belt, and a nervous manner. "Roux," he says. "The house of Roux, in the rue Vivienne, which was once Prisca's and is now, by a quirk of the century, rather my own. My grandfather kept the account and my father kept the account, and I am afraid that I shall have to be the one to tell you that the account is, in one respect, somewhat larger than it was."
  ?end
?end
<~

# lead_waking
?if _lead == "fiammetta"
  The person holding the lantern is small and dark and quick, in a gray traveling dress with the sleeves pushed back, and she has the look of someone who has not stopped working since the last time you saw her. She has your blood, and she has Tommaso's eyes, and she has been seventeen for a very long while. She has also, you learn in the next minute, made herself into a banker. "Sixty five years," says Fiammetta, "and four months. I kept count. Somebody had to. Do not get up. I want you to see the books before you see anything else, because I have been very good and I want it noticed."
?elif _lead == "prisca"
  The person holding the lantern is small and exact, in a traveling cloak of excellent cloth, with a steel pen behind one ear and an abacus in her sleeve. She does not breathe. She has not, for some time, bothered to pretend to. "Sixty five years and four months," says Prisca. "It is a very awkward number. I have kept the books for the whole of it and I should like it understood that the house is the better for it, and that I have enjoyed perhaps one afternoon."
?elif _lead == "thibault"
  The person holding the lantern is a long, quiet man of a hundred years, grown into his hands, in a good black coat with ink on the cuff, and he has been waiting for you to wake for so long that he has rehearsed what to say and forgotten it. "Sixty five years," says Thibault Marchand. "I have the figures. I have not much else prepared. I was a cooper's boy, and I find that I am a banker, and I cannot say which of us is more astonished."
?elif _lead == "hanno"
  The person holding the lantern is a gaunt, patient man in a very old coat, with a pen behind his ear and a small book in his hand, and he says nothing at all for a long moment. Then he opens the book to a page marked with a ribbon, turns it round so that you can read it, and points. It is a sum. It is, you realize, the sum of what the house is worth, written out in a hand like a row of iron nails, and it is a good deal larger than it was.
?elif _lead == "tiro"
  The person holding the lantern is young in the face and not at all young in the eyes, in a plain merchant's coat, with the iron bound purse of the Merchant house at his belt. "Sixty five years," says Tiro. "Prisca taught me that a house is only as honest as its books, and I have kept them like a man who expected to be inspected by her. Welcome back, Master. I have a great deal to show you."
?else
  The person holding the lantern is {_reader}, of your coven, with the look of someone who has been running a house for a long time on instructions left by a person who was asleep, and who is very glad that the person is now awake and can be asked. {_reader} bows, and does not wait to be spoken to. "Sixty five years," {pg(_lead, "he", "she", "they")} says. "The books are in the next room, and so is a chair."
?end
<~

# desk
## What was done while you slept
$ b4.lead_desk = "approved"
When the Emperor has been disposed of, and the money, {_reader} puts three papers on the table, squares them with one finger, and waits to be told that you are ready.
?if wake("place") == "collesanto"
  The first is the charter of the banking house in Florence, which {_reader} established in your name and the house's, with a vault, a brass plate, nine clerks and a reputation for never having lost a deposit. It stands, {pg(_lead, "he", "she", "they")} says, on the credit of the hill.
?else
  The first is the charter of the banking house in the rue Vivienne, which {_reader} established in your name and the house's, with a vault, a brass plate, nine clerks and a reputation for never having lost a deposit. It stands, {pg(_lead, "he", "she", "they")} says, on the credit of the shop.
?end
The second is a bundle of letters from Aurélien Valère of the Rue Royale, eleven years of them, each answered by return. {_reader} has made the two houses, by post, a single counting table, and has done it so quietly that the Prefect's own banker has not noticed. "The Valère know that the master of the Colline is alive and keeps strange hours," says {_reader}. "They believe that I speak for you in everything that is not a secret. Which, as of tonight, is a thing that you may correct."
The third is a list, in two columns, headed *Offers*. Three prospects from the river, each put to the house by Aurélien and none of them yet answered: a share in a line of steamboats, a larger stake in the Valère bank, and a school fund for the free families of the Faubourg. {_reader} has not chosen between them. "It is your money, and your name," {_reader} says. "I have only made sure that you are offered."
:the_choice
* Approve the banking house and the correspondence as written, and thank {_reader} in so many words
  $ b4.lead_desk = "approved"
  $ standing += 1
  {_reader} receives it with the expression of someone who has been hoping for exactly four words and has been given a speech. It is not a great thing, to say that a thing was well done. It is a great deal more than most of the world ever hears.
* Take the third paper first, and choose among the offers
  $ b4.lead_desk = "picked"
  $ cunning_brutal += 1
  ~> offers
* Go through every paper yourself, and send each back marked in the margin
  $ b4.lead_desk = "pressed"
  $ cunning_brutal += 1
  $ standing -= 1
  It takes the rest of the quarter hour and a good deal more. {_reader} answers every mark in the margin, steadily and without being asked twice, and at the end of it you have found three small things to correct and one large thing that was exactly right. It is not an insult. It is, you suspect, very nearly what {_reader} expected, and a little of what {_reader} wanted.
<~

# offers
## The offers
:the_offers
$ _mp_n = 0
$ _mp_rank = 0
:mp_more_1
* The steamboats, the long boats of the river [once]
  $ _mp_n += 1
  ?if _mp_rank < 1
    $ b4.lead_pick = "boats"
    $ _mp_rank = 1
  ?end
  $ do earn(-2)
  {_reader} writes it down. They pay nine, and they pay in a way that makes a person sleep. They also carry cotton, and what is carried with it, and you hear, in your own thoughts, the first small creak of a thing that you will have to answer for.
* The bank, a larger stake in the Valère house [once]
  $ _mp_n += 1
  ?if _mp_rank < 2
    $ b4.lead_pick = "bank"
    $ _mp_rank = 2
  ?end
  $ standing += 1
  {_reader} writes it down. A bank pays eight and asks no one what they do with the money, which is a sort of virtue and a sort of vice. It would put the family in the Valère counting house as partners, and not merely in the post.
* The school fund, for the free families [once]
  $ _mp_n += 1
  ?if _mp_rank < 3
    $ b4.lead_pick = "school"
    $ _mp_rank = 3
  ?end
  $ humanity += 1
  $ do earn(-1)
  {_reader} writes it down, and for a moment the pen stops. It is the one that pays nothing. It is also, says {_reader}, the one that the Valère wrote about on the fifth page of the eleventh letter, with a sentence that was crossed out and then written again.
* That is the whole of it ?if _mp_n > 0
  -> :mp_done_1
-> :mp_more_1
:mp_done_1
<~

# trouble
## Interrupted
?if wake("by") == "hunters"
  They have seen your eyes open. There is a long, delicate silence, in which a gentleman from Lyon thinks very hard about whether it is the kind of thing that a gentleman does, and the guide, who has been in the quarries for twenty years and has seen nearly everything, crosses himself and says that he would like to go home.
?elif wake("by") == "government"
  The commissioner has seen your eyes open, and he writes something down at once, as if that could make it safer. It is a good deal of paper, for a man in a bed.
?else
  {_reader} has seen your eyes open, and has gone the color of milk. Down the passage, on the stair, somebody is coming whom the steward did not mention.
?end
:the_choice
* Slip out in the dark, and leave them an empty bed
  $ b4.trouble = "fled"
  $ secrecy += 1
  It is the oldest of the answers, and you are good at it. The dark has not changed. There is a way out through the old drain, which is not on any plan, and by the time that the lamp has found the bed you are on a wet stair, under a street that is no longer there, with your heart not beating and your coat over your arm.
* Put out the lamp, and be a thing in the dark that they are sorry to have met
  $ b4.trouble = "fought"
  $ cunning_brutal += 3
  $ do power("minor")
  $ do witness(1)
  It takes less than a minute, and nobody is killed, though the guide will never go underground again, and the gentleman from Lyon will tell the story at dinner for the rest of his life, with the details wrong in your favor. You are not proud of it. You are not sorry, either. It is a very odd feeling to be as strong as you are, and to be so thoroughly out of practice.
* Sit up, and ask them in a pleasant voice what the weather is like
  $ b4.trouble = "talked"
  $ cunning_brutal -= 2
  $ standing += 1
  It is a sentence that has done you good service for a thousand years. It is a good deal harder to say with your whole body made of stone and your throat dry, but the effect is as it always was. There is a pause. There is a rather high, wild laugh. The man at the front, who is the bravest, sits down on the floor, and by the time the steward has found his voice the whole affair has become something that can be explained with money.
-> trouble_end

# trouble_end
<~

# hunger
## The first thing
* Take what the house has kept for you, a young goat ?if wake("outcome") != "woken_early"
  $ b4.first_feed = "animal"
  $ do feed("animal")
  $ humanity += 1
  It is a good goat, and has been fed on chestnuts, and somebody has taken the trouble to give it a name. It is a very strange thing to be waited on. You take it gratefully, and with an apology, and it is the apology that stays with you.
* Ask for a willing arm ?if wake("outcome") != "woken_early"
  $ b4.first_feed = "donor"
  $ do feed("donor")
  $ do earn(-2)
  The house keeps a few for exactly this, you learn, men and women paid well and told that they are being bled by a barber. The one who comes is a cooper with forearms like loaves, who holds out his wrist and studies the ceiling with great attention. "Is it very much?" he says. It is not, and you are careful, and he goes away holding his arm against his chest and looking, if anything, a little disappointed.
* Take one of the passing strangers, who will not know
  $ b4.first_feed = "human"
  $ do feed("human")
  $ humanity -= 2
  $ cunning_brutal += 2
  It is quick, and quiet, and you are very good at it, which is the part that you mind. He is a late revelry in a good hat, walking home by the wrong street, and when he goes on, an hour from now, he will think that he was taken ill. You feel the strength come back like a tide into a dock, and under it the old small cold knowledge that you have done it again.
* Go without, and let it gnaw
  $ b4.first_feed = "none"
  $ humanity += 1
  $ cunning_brutal += 1
  You tell them to wait. It is not wisdom. It is a small, stubborn, bad tempered reluctance to begin the age with an apology, and it is a good deal less comfortable than it sounds.
<~

# report
## Sixty five years
You are taken up out of the dark, and fed, and washed, and set in a chair, and {_reader} sits opposite with a candle and a small thick book and the expression of someone who is going to be extremely brief about a matter of a very great length.
?if _reader == "Prisca"
  "I shall be exact," says Prisca, "since it is the only thing at which I am ever any use."
?end
Sixty five years is a long time to put into a quarter of an hour, and {_reader} does it well. There was a king, and there was no king, and there was a general who made himself an Emperor and marched to Moscow and back and was put on an island, and then there was a king again, who was sent away, and another king, who was sent away, and a Republic that lasted a very short season and was succeeded, by a vote of seven million, by a nephew. The nephew is the Emperor now. He wears a mustache that must be seen to be believed. He has put in a good many boulevards, and put out a good many people who lived where the boulevards are.
"The Emperor," says {_reader}, "has been very kind to the printing trade, which is to say that he has hanged nobody I am fond of."
:the_choice
* Say that you had understood that there was an Emperor already, and ask after his health
  $ b4.emperor = "bluffed"
  $ standing += 1
  "The Emperor is very well," says {_reader}, with the face of someone who has been asked a thing that is not sure to be safe to answer. "He is, of course, the nephew." There is a short, delicate pause. "Of the other one. Who is dead. For thirty eight years." You nod gravely, as a man does who knew it, and the pause gets a little longer.
* Ask which one
  $ b4.emperor = "asked"
  $ adjustment += 2
  "Napoleon," says {_reader}. "The third." You ask what became of the second. {_reader} explains that the second was a child, who was never anything at all, and that the first was the one that you will have heard of, and that you will have to take the numbers on trust. It is, you find, a perfectly tolerable way to learn a century.
* Declare that Bonapartes are a passing thing, like plague, and that you will wait
  $ b4.emperor = "blustered"
  $ cunning_brutal += 1
  $ standing -= 1
  It is a sentence you would have made, in your day, about a number of rulers, and been right nine times in ten. {_reader} agrees, in the manner of someone who has found that it is a good deal safer to agree with this particular customer. You are left with a faint and uneasy impression that you have been, for the first time in your life, the person in the room who was behind the times.
The money, {_reader} says, is the next thing, and is easier.
?if wake("outcome") == "prospered"
  It is a good deal richer than the book said that it should be. The war years, which were hard on everybody, were kind to the house, by a combination of good steward and bad luck for others, and there is a note at the foot of the page that says, with real feeling, that it was not a thing to be proud of and that {_reader} is proud of it all the same.
?elif wake("outcome") == "losses"
  It is not as rich as it should be. The war years were hard on everybody and harder on the house, and there is a column of figures that {_reader} reads in the flat quick voice of someone who has been waiting a long time to be shouted at. You find that you do not wish to shout. A fortune is a living thing, and it was left in a century that had a good deal of fire.
?elif wake("outcome") == "woken_early"
  It is not the money that ought to have been, since the stewardship ended before it was meant to, and there is a good deal that has been left half done. You will have to be a little careful for the next few years, and rather quick.
?else
  It is very nearly what was said. The war years were hard on everybody, and the house came through with a few scratches and the same number of ledgers, and there is something very restful in a thing that has done precisely what it promised.
?end
?if wake("place") == "collesanto"
  The hill, {_reader} says, is the one thing that has not changed, except by growing. There is a great house on it, and a shrine, and a chapel with a bell, and a school that teaches the catechism and a little arithmetic. The Grand Duke has gone, and there is something called a Provisional Government, and the Piedmontese are said to be coming, and nobody on the hill has noticed any of it.
?else
  The shop, {_reader} says, will not last the year. It is on the plan. The Prefect is cutting a new street through the old quarter, and the house is on the line of it, and the compensation is not what a man would call generous. The cellar, which was the oldest part of the building, is on the list to be filled with rubble in the spring.
?end
There is a pause, and a little cough.
"There is also," says {_reader}, "the matter of the letters."
<~

# night
## The first night
:night
?if actions_left() == 3
  The night is young, and so, you think, are you, which is absurd.
?elif actions_left() == 2
  The night has a long way to go, and the lamp has begun to gutter, and {_reader} has begun to look at the clock, which is an object that you are not yet used to.
?else
  There is a gray in the sky that is not yet a color, and the first carts have begun, and you have one thing left that you have time for.
?end
* Go through the books [once] ?if actions_left() > 0
  ~> books
  $ do act()
* Go out, and look at the world [once] ?if actions_left() > 0
  ~> street
  $ do act()
* Gather the household [once] ?if actions_left() > 0
  ~> household
  $ do act()
* Open the letters [once] ?if actions_left() > 0
  ~> letters
  $ do act()
?if actions_left() <= 0
  $ do end_phase()
  -> dawn
?end
-> :night

# books
## The ledger
{_reader} has laid them out on the table, three of them, in a kind of descending order of bulk, and sits very still while you read.
?if wake("orders") == "dark"
  The first is the long ledger of what was done in your name, and it is a hard thing to read, because it is so clean. Over sixty years the Valère cousins of Louisiana have bought a sugar estate below the city, on the river, a place called Belle Isle, with a mill and a sugar house and a landing, and eleven hundred arpents of cane. It is a very good estate. It pays eleven per cent. It is a thing that you wrote, in a cellar in the rue Saint-Jacques, on a night when you were tired, and called an investment. The last column is headed *Hands*. It has a number at the foot, and there are names on the page above the number, each with a figure in the margin, and the figures are prices.
  There are two hundred and six of them. Somebody has written, in a small neat hand at the bottom, *As the law requires*.
  You read the names. It takes a long time. A good many of them are children.
  * Say that nothing is to be changed until you have seen it for yourself
    $ b4.estate = "held"
    $ humanity -= 1
    $ standing += 1
    {_reader} writes it down. It is the sort of instruction that costs nothing, and does, for the present, exactly the thing that you most dislike, which is to leave two hundred and six people where they are. You tell yourself that it is for a month. You tell yourself that it is the most prudent course. You know the sound of the voice that tells you.
  * Order the whole estate sold, land and mill and all, to the first honest buyer
    $ b4.estate = "sale"
    $ humanity -= 3
    $ do earn(30)
    $ cunning_brutal += 2
    You say it, and {_reader} writes it, and you both look at the sentence for a moment. It is what is called a clean decision. The land, and the mill, and the hands, go to the next man, and you are out of it, and richer, and the people are on a different page of someone else's book. It does not, you find, feel clean. It feels like a thing that you have done with your sleeve over your face.
  * Order the house's lawyer to find out every means there is of freeing them, and to write at once
    $ b4.estate = "inquiry"
    $ humanity += 2
    $ standing -= 1
    $ do earn(-3)
    "There are none," says {_reader}, at once, and then, rather more slowly, "that are legal." There is a law, it seems, of the year before last, that forbids a master in Louisiana to free any man at any price, in any manner, for any cause. There are, {_reader} adds, a few things that are done in the margins. They involve the legislature, and Ohio, and a great deal of money, and are rather often unsuccessful. You tell the lawyer to begin.
?elif wake("orders") == "aggressive"
  The first is the long ledger of what was done in your name, and it is bold, and handsome, and rather alarming. The cousins of Louisiana, the Valère of the Rue Royale, have set up a bank in your name, and a counting house, and bought shares in eleven steamboats and a canal, and lent a good deal of money to a great many planters on mortgage. The mortgages are listed at the back. They are listed, as the custom of the country is, by the land and by what stands on the land, and what stands on the land is described in a line, as a man might describe a mule.
  You close the book. It is a bank. It lends money on the security that is offered. It is also, you notice, owed a very great deal by a great many people who are not going to pay.
?else
  The first is the long ledger of what was done in your name, and it is the plainest that you have ever read. Rents, and farms, and a few good houses in Paris with good walls, and a share in the Valère counting house in Louisiana, which has done quite well in cotton factorage and has been, the steward remarks, very careful to be as dull as possible. It is a pleasing thing, a fortune that has kept its head down. You find yourself wondering, as a man does, if it is quite honest to be so comfortable.
?end
The second ledger is the house's own, the running account. The third is a small book, bound in green, which is the Valère's private note of what has been done *for* you, and not in your name, and it is not very long.
:the_choice
* Take the books on trust, and thank {_reader}
  $ b4.books = "trusted"
  $ bond.ginevra += 0
  $ standing += 1
  {_reader} lets out a long breath, and looks very much younger. It is a little thing, to say that you trust a man. You find that it is worth rather more than the interest.
* Go through them line by line, and ask what is not written
  $ b4.books = "audited"
  $ cunning_brutal += 1
  It takes the whole of an hour. {_reader} answers every question, steadily, and without being asked twice, and at the end of it you are quite sure that nothing has been hidden, and that you have hurt the feelings of an honest man. It is a thing that you will do again. The balance of the world, you reflect, is a great deal of hurt feelings against a fair amount of hidden money.
-> books_end

# books_end
$ do discover("the_crescent_city")
?if can_build("infirmary") or can_build("granary") or wealth > 0
  ~> hill_letter
?end
<~

# hill_letter
## A letter from the hill
Between the second ledger and the third there is a letter that the books did not account for, in the careful hand of the keepers of the hill, with a thumbprint in the corner where someone has been holding the paper down against the wind. The village is well, it says, and has grown, and has a good many more mouths than it had when you lay down. It does not ask for anything. It says only that the winter was hard, and that the old women have been saying, in the church porch, that it would be a mercy if the hill had somewhere to put the sick that was not a barn.
:the_hill
$ _mp_n = 0
$ _mp_rank = 0
:mp_more_2
* Pay for an infirmary on the hill, with a physician's room and a ward [once] ?if can_build("infirmary")
  $ _mp_n += 1
  ?if _mp_rank < 2
    $ b4.village = "built"
    $ _mp_rank = 2
  ?end
  $ do build("infirmary")
  $ humanity += 1
  You write the sum, and sign it, and it goes into the post as a thing that costs a great deal less than it seems. It is a long, low, whitewashed building, in the end, with eight beds and a stove, and a woman from the village who has been a midwife for thirty years to run it. It will be there long after the thing that built it has been forgotten, and that, you reflect, is nearly the only kind of monument that you have ever liked.
* Pay for a granary against the bad years, with a lock and a ledger [once] ?if can_build("granary")
  $ _mp_n += 1
  ?if _mp_rank < 2
    $ b4.village = "built"
    $ _mp_rank = 2
  ?end
  $ do build("granary")
  $ standing += 1
  It is a dull thing, a granary, and the best investment that you will make this year. The hill will have bread when the valley does not, and a ledger to show who was given what, and a lock whose key is held by three people at once. You are told that the old women have already begun to quarrel about who is to be the third.
* Send coin to the hill, and let the village choose [once] ?if wealth > 0
  $ _mp_n += 1
  ?if _mp_rank < 1
    $ b4.village = "sent"
    $ _mp_rank = 1
  ?end
  $ do send_home(30)
  It is the lazy kindness, and also, often, the wisest. The village will spend it on whatever it most lacks, which will not be what you would have guessed. There will be a letter in the spring that tells you what it was.
* Write that you have read the letter, and that you will think of it ?if _mp_n == 0
  $ b4.village = "waited"
  It is not a refusal. It is something like the opposite, and not quite a promise, and the writer, when it reaches the hill, will know the difference exactly.
  -> :mp_done_2
* That is the whole of it ?if _mp_n > 0
  -> :mp_done_2
-> :mp_more_2
:mp_done_2
