# street
## The new streets
?if _hill == 1
  You go up through the village, in the dark, and it is a thing you would not have known. There are lamps in the windows, of oil, and a lamp in the church, and a great many new walls, and the road is smoother than a road ought to be. There is a boy with a lantern on the wall of the square who looks at you and then very carefully at his shoes, as he has evidently been taught to do.
  There is a notice nailed to the door of the chapel, in a clerk's hand, with a stamp. It says that the Provisional Government of Tuscany has abolished a great many things, and that the village is asked to be calm. Somebody has written under it, in charcoal, *Ma il Custode resta*.
?else
  You go out by the shop door, and you stand in the street, and it is not the street. The old crooked lane has been opened at one end into a great raw gash, forty feet wide, with a gas lamp every thirty paces and a wall of new white stone going up on each side, and the whole of it smells of lime and horses and wet iron. There are cafés with their tables out, in November, under glass, and men reading newspapers in the lamplight, and an omnibus going by with a lit lamp on each end like a lit ship.
  It is a great deal of light. You stand and let it go over you. You find, to your surprise, that you are not frightened. You are, if anything, rather taken by it. It is a city built by a man who wished to be able to see his enemies from a long way off.
?end
There is a newspaper, as there is always, and a man to read it aloud, as there is not always.
:the_choice
* Take a paper, and read the news from America
  $ b4.street = "read"
  $ adjustment += 3
  There is a great deal, in a very small print. A man with a beard and a cause has seized an arsenal in Virginia with eighteen followers, and has been taken, and is to be tried, and the whole of the American South has apparently taken it as a declaration of war. There is a long column on the Union, and what is said to be coming apart in it, and a shorter one on the price of cotton, which is at its highest in twenty years. You read it twice. You find that the world, whatever it has done in your absence, has not changed its habit of going wrong in the same place.
* Talk to the man who is reading it, and ask what he thinks
  $ b4.street = "spoke"
  $ standing += 1
  $ adjustment += 2
  He is an American painter of the middling sort, with a very good opinion of the Seine and a very bad one of his own work, and he tells you, over a glass that he has not been asked to share, that it is all coming. He says that his people in Georgia have stopped talking about whether, and begun to talk about when. He says it with a sort of sad enjoyment, like a man describing a bridge that he has seen about to fall. "You should go," he says, "if you are going. If you are going at all. It is going to be the greatest thing that has happened in a hundred years."
* Walk on, and watch the faces
  $ b4.street = "watched"
  $ cunning_brutal -= 1
  $ adjustment += 2
  It is the old art, and a good one. You watch the faces go by in the gaslight and the shapes of the coats and what is carried, and after half an hour you have learned that there are a great many more of everything than there were, and that the poor have grown, if anything, a little thinner, and that nobody at all has looked up, once, to see who you were.
$ do discover("the_second_empire")
