# household
## Your people
?if coven_size() > 0
  They have been waiting, as the family does, in the room above, and when you come up the stair there is a small, unmistakable hush, of the sort that follows a person who has been missed. It is not a thing that you are good at. You find that you do not mind.
  ?if in_circle("prisca") and member("prisca", "status") == "active"
    Prisca is at the end of the table, with her abacus, pretending to be reading.
  ?end
  ?if in_circle("fiammetta") and member("fiammetta", "status") == "active"
    Fiammetta has put herself where she can see the door, and has not, you notice, put her hands in her lap, as she did in Florence, but on the table, flat, the way a person does who has been holding a long thing and has put it down.
  ?end
  ?if in_circle("thibault") and member("thibault", "status") == "active"
    Thibault is in the corner, grown into his hands, as they do after a century, and looks at you as if he were waiting to be told that he is not allowed to be glad.
  ?end
  ?if in_circle("hanno") and member("hanno", "status") == "active"
    Hanno has a pen and a very small book, and says, you are sure, nothing.
  ?end
  ?if _lead != ""
    {_reader} has the head of the table, as someone does who has had it for sixty years, with the books open at {pg(_lead, "his", "her", "their")} elbow and a bundle of letters from Louisiana beside them. It is {_reader} who tells you what the others only look: that the house has been writing to the Valère of the river for eleven years, that the Valère know the master's name only as a very old friend of the house, and that the family has made up its mind to go if you go.
  ?end
  There is a map, with a river on it, drawn by someone who has never seen a river that size. They have been told where you are going, and they have views.
  :the_choice
  * Take them all with you, and let the family come to the river
    $ b4.circle = "all"
    $ do discover("the_river_covens")
    $ standing += 1
    It is a large decision, and a risky one. A coven that comes ashore all at once is a thing that a city will notice. It is also, you think, a very different thing from a man alone. You tell them that you will not have anybody left in a cellar. A silence follows, of the peculiar kind in which a number of very old people are being, at the same time, pleased. You will need a house for them, and a good deal of care.
  * Go first, and let them follow when you have found them a roof
    $ b4.circle = "ahead"
    $ cunning_brutal += 1
    You tell them that you will send for them, and that they are to be, until then, the arm that you have left in Europe: your eyes, and your counting house, and the thing that does not sleep when you do. There is a short protest, and then, which is better, a quick, practical silence, in which the work is being divided. It is a thing that is easy to ask of a family. It is much harder to keep asking.
  * Leave them here, with the business and the hill, and go alone
    $ b4.circle = "stay"
    $ cunning_brutal += 2
    $ humanity -= 1
    You say that the river is not a place for the whole of the house, and that it is a long way, and a young country, and a very good deal of it is on fire. All of this is true. It is also the answer of a man who has begun to think that the family is a weakness. You see them take it, one by one, with an old courtesy, and not one of them looks surprised, which is the worst of it.
?else
  There is no coven. There is the house, which is a good deal more of a thing than you had counted on, and has met you, in the room above, with a quiet that you recognize as the quiet of a family that has been waiting for a very long time. There is a table. There is a pot of coffee, and a plate of rolls that nobody has touched, and eleven people of every age from a nursing infant to an old woman in a cap, and every one of them has Aelia's chin, or has had it passed down in some other form.
  The oldest says, in the voice of a woman who has not been asked for her opinion since before the Republic, that the keepers of the house have talked it over and that somebody is going with you.
  :the_keepers
  * Let a keeper go with you, a young one, as a secretary
    $ b4.circle = "all"
    $ do discover("the_river_covens")
    $ standing += 1
    $ humanity += 1
    They send a young man of nineteen, a clerk, with a small bag and a very good hand and a look of pure terror held in place by pure determination. You tell him that he is not to call you Master. He says that he will try not to. By the end of the evening he has called you Monsieur Colline with a hard, practical emphasis, as a child holds a thing that he has been told is hot.
  * Ask them to write, and to hold the house, and go alone
    $ b4.circle = "stay"
    $ cunning_brutal += 1
    You say that it is not a thing to ask of a family, to cross a sea for a man who may not be, at the end, a very good cause. The old woman looks at you for a long time. She says that the family has been crossing seas for you since before there were ships to cross them in, and that it has noticed that you never ask. She says it quite kindly. She says that it is a fault, and not a small one.
?end
<~

# letters
## The letters
There are two, and the second is on better paper than the first.
$ do meet("aurelien")
$ do meet("crane")
The first is from New Orleans, from the Rue Royale, in a clear upright commercial hand, and is signed by a cousin of the house whom you do not know. His name is Aurélien Valère. He writes to the master of the house, in a French that has been a little rubbed by the river, to say that the family of Louisiana has done its best, that it has been nine and forty years in that country, and that there is a matter which cannot be put in a letter.
> There is a gentleman who calls at the counting house at ten o'clock on the first Friday of the month. He asks, very civilly, whether the Colline of the rue Saint-Jacques has woken. He says that the house of Beaumont would be honored. We have said that we do not know the name. He said that he did not think that we were very good at it.
> I do not say that you should come. I say only that there are four hundred thousand people in Louisiana, of whom a third are free and a third are not, and that a great many of them are afraid, and that I believe that this is not the usual sort of fear.
?if _lead != ""
  Folded into it is a note in {_reader}'s own hand, which has the speed and economy of someone who writes a letter a day: *I have written to him every month for eleven years. He is honest, frightened and very good at figures. Believe the second and third, and be gentle with the first. I have told him nothing of what you are. That is yours to say, or not to say, to his face.*
?end
The second is on thick cream paper, from New York, with a Pearl Street direction and a small, neat, sarcastic hand. It has been in the book of the house for a year and a day. It is signed *H. Crane*.
?if b3.crane_offer == "considered"
  > My dear Colline, I hold the island, as you know, in a manner of speaking, and I cannot think why it should not be held a little more handsomely if we were to speak. It has been a very pleasant sixty years. I should have said so before, but you were not, I understand, in a condition to receive me.
?elif b3.crane_offer == "refused"
  > My dear Colline, I was sorry that you would not sell me the island, and glad, I confess, that you did not. A man ought to be somewhat difficult to buy. It makes the buying worth more.
?else
  > My dear Colline, I hear that you are asleep again, as is your habit. I can only hope that you have chosen a pleasant place and a quiet century. I keep a house on Pearl Street, and a table, and a very good opinion of anybody who can stay awake through a hundred years of the Bourbons.
?end
> The weather in the South is said to be lovely at this time of year. It is going to turn, I believe. You will find the North less picturesque but a good deal more reliable. The door is always open, and I have been told that I have a way of leaving it so. Do come when you are rested. H. C.
:the_choice
* Burn it. A man who writes like that wants to be answered, and you will not
  $ b4.crane_reply = "burned"
  $ cunning_brutal += 1
  It is a good letter, and it curls well. You watch it go, and wonder, as you do it, whether there is any kind of silence that is not, itself, an answer.
* Keep it, and say nothing
  $ b4.crane_reply = "kept"
  $ do discover("the_river_covens")
  It goes into the green book, between a laundry bill and a recipe for a tincture, where it will stay. It is a very small thing to do. It is, you suspect, quite exactly what the writer wishes.
* Answer it, politely, and leave a door ajar
  $ b4.crane_reply = "answered"
  $ standing += 2
  $ secrecy -= 1
  You write four lines, in a hand that you have not used since the Peace, and you are careful to say nothing at all. It is a pleasure to be so exact about nothing. {_reader} carries it to the post, with the expression of one who is posting a live bird.
Then there is the matter of the passage. The Valère of the river have sent a draft on a house at Le Havre, and a name, and a berth.
:the_passage
* Go as a gentleman in the cabin, with a trunk and a valet and a letter of introduction
  $ b4.passage = "cabin"
  $ do earn(-3)
  $ standing += 1
  It is the safe thing, and the dull one. It will put you at a table with a number of strangers for forty days, who will all want to know what you think about the Union.
* Go below, in the hold, and let nobody ask you anything
  $ b4.passage = "hold"
  $ secrecy += 1
  $ humanity -= 1
  It is a place that you know. A hold is a hold in any age. You tell the master that you are a consumptive, and a recluse, and that you will pay him double to be forgotten, which is the one thing that a master of a ship understands completely.
* Be freight. Have them crate the box, and ship it as a piece of the cargo
  $ b4.passage = "cargo"
  $ cunning_brutal += 1
  $ secrecy += 1
  It is the oldest way, and a ridiculous one, and you cannot help being rather proud of it. A great wooden crate, marked *Printing Types*, with a name in a clerk's hand, will go into the hold of a cotton packet next to a number of pianos. The steward is perfectly sure that it will be safe, and equally sure that you are mad.
<~

# dawn
## The road to the sea
?if _hill == 1
  $ do chart("collesanto4.road")
  The light comes up the valley, pink and gray, and the first bell begins on the hill, and you are in the coach before the village is properly awake. There is a small crowd at the door of the chapel, very quiet, with a boy at the front holding a lantern, and you are not at all sure that it is for you. You raise your hat. A woman in a cap makes the sign of the cross, quickly, as at a funeral, and a child waves. It is a very long road to the sea, by Pisa and Livorno, and a boat to Marseille, and the railway up through the Rhône, and a good deal of the time is going to be spent looking out of windows at a world that has been, while you were not looking, cut into straight lines.
?else
  The light comes, at the top of the new street, a pale streak with a fringe of smoke, and the first bell of the Sorbonne begins, and over the roof of the shop there is the pink fan of the sunrise, which you do not look at for long. A cab is waiting at the door, with a cabman in a glazed hat who has been told that he is to drive a gentleman to the Gare Saint-Lazare, and is wondering, with the instinct of his trade, why the gentleman has no hat. Behind you the shop, which is going to come down in the spring, shows a number in red paint on its door. You look at it. You have the feeling, which you have had before, of leaving a place in which you were fond of being a man.
?end
Somewhere beyond the sea there is a river that you have never seen, and a city that is said to sit lower than the water that feeds it, and a gentleman who calls at ten o'clock on the first Friday of the month. The train to Le Havre leaves at noon.
$ do rest()
